1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,145
Aarav turns the volume knob on a speaker.

2
00:00:03,145 --> 00:00:06,373
The music grows louder,
smoothly, with no steps.

3
00:00:06,723 --> 00:00:08,284
What is behind that knob?

4
00:00:08,784 --> 00:00:09,700
A resistor.

5
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:12,419
A part that opposes the flow of current.

6
00:00:12,769 --> 00:00:15,209
Its resistance is measured in ohms.

7
00:00:15,209 --> 00:00:18,611
More ohms means less current
for the same voltage.

8
00:00:18,961 --> 00:00:20,839
There are two big families.

9
00:00:20,839 --> 00:00:23,666
Fixed resistors, whose
value never changes.

10
00:00:23,666 --> 00:00:27,007
And variable resistors,
whose value you can change.

11
00:00:27,657 --> 00:00:30,002
Fixed resistors come in a few kinds.

12
00:00:30,352 --> 00:00:34,997
Carbon composition resistors are made
of carbon powder mixed with a binder.

13
00:00:34,997 --> 00:00:37,576
They are cheap, but not very precise.

14
00:00:37,926 --> 00:00:42,789
Carbon film and metal film resistors
have a thin layer coated on a ceramic

15
00:00:42,789 --> 00:00:43,689
rod.

16
00:00:43,330 --> 00:00:46,604
Metal film is the more
precise and stable one.

17
00:00:46,954 --> 00:00:51,631
Wire wound resistors use a long wire,
often nichrome, wound on a ceramic

18
00:00:51,631 --> 00:00:52,531
core.

19
00:00:52,196 --> 00:00:55,358
They can handle large
power without burning out.

20
00:00:56,008 --> 00:00:57,599
Now the variable ones.

21
00:00:57,949 --> 00:01:00,233
A potentiometer has three terminals.

22
00:01:00,233 --> 00:01:04,734
Two ends of a resistive track, and
a sliding contact called the wiper.

23
00:01:05,084 --> 00:01:09,759
A rheostat uses only two terminals,
one end and the wiper, to change the

24
00:01:09,759 --> 00:01:10,659
current.

25
00:01:10,817 --> 00:01:13,286
A preset, or trimmer, is a tiny one.

26
00:01:13,286 --> 00:01:16,685
It is set once with a
screwdriver, and then left alone.

27
00:01:17,335 --> 00:01:20,346
Let's build the volume
control on the breadboard.

28
00:01:20,346 --> 00:01:24,097
A potentiometer, with the music
signal across its two ends.

29
00:01:24,447 --> 00:01:28,051
The wiper picks off part of the
signal and sends it to the speaker.

30
00:01:28,401 --> 00:01:29,618
Watch the scope.

31
00:01:29,618 --> 00:01:31,705
Channel one is the full input.

32
00:01:31,705 --> 00:01:34,346
Channel two is what reaches the speaker.

33
00:01:34,696 --> 00:01:35,989
Turn the knob up.

34
00:01:35,989 --> 00:01:38,914
The wiper slides, and
the output wave grows.

35
00:01:38,914 --> 00:01:40,449
The music gets louder.

36
00:01:40,799 --> 00:01:43,044
Turn it down, and the output shrinks.

37
00:01:43,044 --> 00:01:45,074
At the bottom end, it is silent.

38
00:01:45,724 --> 00:01:47,695
Fixed resistors set a value.

39
00:01:47,695 --> 00:01:50,183
Variable resistors let you choose it.

40
00:01:50,983 --> 00:01:53,309
So how much does the wiper pick off?

41
00:01:53,309 --> 00:01:54,542
Let's work it out.

42
00:01:54,892 --> 00:01:58,686
The resistance of a uniform
track grows with its length.

43
00:01:58,686 --> 00:02:00,671
So R is proportional to L.

44
00:02:01,371 --> 00:02:06,592
If the wiper sits a length x from the
bottom, the lower part has a resistance

45
00:02:06,592 --> 00:02:08,992
R x equals x over L, times R.

46
00:02:09,692 --> 00:02:13,434
The current through the whole
track is I equals V in over R.

47
00:02:14,134 --> 00:02:17,617
The output is that current
times the lower part.

48
00:02:17,617 --> 00:02:20,684
So V out equals V in times R x over R.

49
00:02:21,384 --> 00:02:23,641
This is called a voltage divider.

50
00:02:23,641 --> 00:02:27,754
Volts in volts, ohms in ohms,
and the fraction has no unit.

51
00:02:28,404 --> 00:02:29,711
Worked example.

52
00:02:29,711 --> 00:02:34,202
A ten kilo ohm potentiometer
is connected across nine volts.

53
00:02:34,552 --> 00:02:39,381
The wiper is at thirty percent of
the track, so R x is three kilo ohms.

54
00:02:39,731 --> 00:02:42,763
The current is nine
divided by ten thousand.

55
00:02:42,763 --> 00:02:45,309
That is zero point nine milliamperes.

56
00:02:45,909 --> 00:02:48,500
V out is nine times three over ten.

57
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:50,162
Two point seven volts.

58
00:02:50,812 --> 00:02:52,277
Now, the exam trap.

59
00:02:52,627 --> 00:02:57,108
Many students think a potentiometer
and a rheostat are connected the same

60
00:02:57,108 --> 00:02:58,008
way.

61
00:02:57,625 --> 00:02:58,719
They are not.

62
00:02:59,069 --> 00:03:03,572
A potentiometer uses all three
terminals and divides the voltage.

63
00:03:03,572 --> 00:03:07,406
A rheostat uses two terminals
and changes the current.

64
00:03:08,056 --> 00:03:09,701
Where is this asked?

65
00:03:09,701 --> 00:03:11,347
In PUC board papers.

66
00:03:11,347 --> 00:03:16,493
Classify resistors, describe a wire
wound resistor, or tell a potentiometer

67
00:03:16,493 --> 00:03:17,852
from a rheostat.

68
00:03:18,502 --> 00:03:19,402
Try this.

69
00:03:19,355 --> 00:03:23,620
A twenty kilo ohm potentiometer
is connected across twelve volts.

70
00:03:23,620 --> 00:03:27,386
The wiper is a quarter of
the way up from the bottom.

71
00:03:27,386 --> 00:03:31,942
A five kilo ohm load is connected
between the wiper and the bottom.

72
00:03:31,942 --> 00:03:33,700
Find the output voltage.

73
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:40,200
B.

74
00:03:39,767 --> 00:03:42,283
About one point seven one volts.

75
00:03:42,633 --> 00:03:46,743
The lower part is five kilo ohms,
and the upper part is fifteen.

76
00:03:47,093 --> 00:03:50,299
The load sits in parallel
with the lower part.

77
00:03:50,299 --> 00:03:54,148
Five and five in parallel
make two point five kilo ohms.

78
00:03:54,498 --> 00:03:58,895
V out is twelve times two point
five over seventeen point five.

79
00:03:58,895 --> 00:04:01,809
That is about one point seven one volts.

80
00:04:02,459 --> 00:04:03,980
Screenshot this card.

81
00:04:07,180 --> 00:04:09,805
Next, resistor colour codes and ratings.

82
00:04:09,805 --> 00:04:12,797
How do four coloured
bands tell you the value?
